e	op_	au$, witnessed by $ ot_	au$ but impossible of $c$; Separated Structure fails since $\lambda x\, .\,x$ and $\lambda x\, .\,	op_	au$ agree on $c$ and differ; Independence (signature Σ) fails since $c$ denotes what the closed pure term $	op_	au$ denotes; Distinctness Maximalism (signature Σ) fails since $c=	op_	au$ is a true identity that C(Σ) does not prove. Everything that implies these fails with them. Further facts: the root proposition, true at the root alone, is an atom entailing every root truth, so Actuality holds. BF holds at the root: an entity of a relational type there is a family of component entities with a free root component, every component entity is the component of some root entity, so if $ orall y\, .\,\Box Xy$ holds at the root then every component of $X$ is universal and so is its root component, whence $\Box orall y\, .\,Xy$; BF holds at the one-world components trivially, so $\Box$BF holds. No Pure Contingency fails, since the pure sentence that there are at least two individuals is true at the root and false at the first component. Possibility Maximalism (pure) fails, since Atomlessness is consistent with C but false at every world: each component is a one-world model and the root proposition is an atom. The type-t Strong Leibniz Biconditionals hold necessarily: at the root, the singleton of the arrow to component $n$ becomes that component’s identity under it and empty under every other arrow, and the root proposition becomes empty under every non-identity arrow, so every singleton is a strong world; the components are one-world models. Here the components are full Henkin models, so every set of arrows is a root proposition: the root propositions form a complete atomic algebra, giving Atomicity and Boolean Completeness at type t, and Relational Choice holds at the root by a functional choice in the free root component. Possibility+ (pure) fails: the component with three individuals has three at its only world; take two root individuals that differ at that component and agree everywhere else; the pure formula saying that $x
e y$ and there are exactly two individuals is consistent, but at the root there are infinitely many individuals, under every arrow into another component the two collapse, and under the arrow into that component there are three. □Relational Choice holds: Relational Choice holds at the root by the free root component and at every component, each being a full Henkin model. Distinctness-preserving collapse holds: let $a$ be the proposition true at the actual world alone (the identity arrow). For any true $p$, take $q:=a$ in the definition of $\Box_{
e}p$: under a non-identity arrow $i$, $\Diamond a$ would need an arrow $k$ with $k\circ i$ the identity, and none exists, since the components have no arrows to the root; under the identity, $p$ holds. So every truth is $\Box_{
e}$-necessary, although ND fails. The lattice principles hold at the root at every relational type, and necessarily. At a type $\sigma	o t$ a root entity is a pair $(G_0,(G_n)_n)$ with $G_0$ any function on the root domain and $G_n$ in the full domain of $M_n$; entailment is componentwise. Atomicity: below a nonzero $G$ lies the atom that is true at the root of one argument $a$ with $G_0(a)=1$ and bottom in every component, or, if $G_0=0$, the atom that is bottom at the root and in all but one component $M_n$, where it is an atom of that full model below $G_n$. Both kinds of atom are strong worlds: at the root they entail every entity or its negation, being singletons in one coordinate and bottom elsewhere, and their shifts to the components are bottom or atoms of a one-world full model. So the Strong Leibniz Biconditionals hold at every type. Boolean Completeness: the componentwise meet of any set of root entities is a root entity and is the greatest lower bound. Rigid Comprehension: given $X$ with root extension $E$, let $Y$ have root component $X_0$ and, in $M_n$, the projection of $E$ to the $n$-th coordinates; $Y$ is persistent, since a root instance has its projection in every component, and inextensible, since a relation necessary of every root instance contains those projections, while in the one-world components persistence and inextensibility are automatic. Each component is a full Henkin model, in which all of these hold, so the boxed forms hold at the root; Countable Boolean Completeness, the weak rigid comprehension principles and Necessity of Arithmetic follow by the recorded results. B for pure sentences fails, and B for sentences of $\Sigma$ with it: the pure sentence that there are two individuals is true at the root and false at the first component, from which no arrow returns. Observations of Claude Fable 5.1 (Anthropic), 25 September 2026.

